<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>dynamics</title></head><body><h1>dynamics</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Linear Momentum and Impulse</li><li>Conservation of Linear Momentum</li><li>Conservation of Linear Momentum</li><li>Application of Newton's Laws</li><li>Application of Newton's Laws</li><li>Moment, Torque and Equilibrium</li><li>Moment, Torque and Equilibrium</li><li>Solid Friction — Laws and Verification</li><li>Solid Friction: Laws and Verification</li><li>Solid Friction</li></ul><h2>Linear Momentum and Impulse</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Type 2 — alternate treatment:** this version emphasises the momentum form of Newton's second law and the impulse–momentum theorem with force–time graphs — the framing NEB theory questions use.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Impulsive Force:** A force that acts for a very short time but produces a finite change in momentum. Examples: hammer hitting a nail, bat hitting a ball, foot kicking a football. The force is extremely large, often thousands of Newtons, but the duration is milliseconds. The impulse is the area under the force-time curve, $J = \int F dt$.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Linear momentum:** $\vec{p} = m\vec{v}$ — mass in motion, SI unit kg·m/s. It is a VECTOR along the velocity. A 1 kg ball at 10 m/s and a 10 kg cart at 1 m/s carry equal momentum but very different kinetic energies ($\frac{1}{2}mv^2$: 50 J vs 5 J). Momentum is a measure of the 'difficulty' to stop an object. It is the 'inertia of motion'.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Physical Significance:** Momentum is fundamentally linked to the translational symmetry of space (Noether's theorem). In quantum mechanics, momentum is related to the wave vector $k$ by $p = \hbar k$.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Conservation:** Momentum is conserved in any system where the net external force is zero. This is a universal law, holding from subatomic particles to galactic collisions.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Newton's second law in momentum form:** $\vec{F} = \dfrac{d\vec{p}}{dt} = \dfrac{d(m\vec{v})}{dt}$. For constant mass this reduces to $\vec{F} = m\vec{a}$ — but the momentum form is the GENERAL law and is the version that stays valid for variable-mass systems (rockets).</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Impulse:** $\vec{J} = \vec{F}\,\Delta t$ for a constant force, and $\vec{J} = \int \vec{F}\,dt$ in general — the AREA under a force–time graph. Impulse has units N·s = kg·m/s, the same as momentum — by design.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Impulse–momentum theorem:** $\vec{J} = \Delta\vec{p} = m\vec{v}_f - m\vec{v}_i$. Any desired momentum change can be had with a LARGE force for a short time or a SMALL force for a long time — the product is what matters.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Why airbags/crumple zones work:** to stop a passenger, $\Delta p$ is fixed by the crash; extending the collision time $\Delta t$ cuts the average force $F_{avg} = \Delta p/\Delta t$. Bending your knees on landing, catching a cricket ball with draw-back hands, and jumping with flexed legs are all the same physics.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Worked example:** a 0.15 kg cricket ball at 30 m/s is stopped in 0.1 s. $\Delta p = 0.15 \times 30 = 4.5$ kg·m/s → $F_{avg} = 45$ N. If caught in 0.02 s: 225 N — five times the force from the same momentum change.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**Variable force:** when $F(t)$ is a curve, impulse is the area under it; for $F = F_0 e^{-t/\tau}$-style impacts, $J = F_0\tau$. NEB usually gives triangular or rectangular force–time pulses — count the areas.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** define momentum and impulse with units; state and use $F = dp/dt$; compute impulse from force–time graphs; apply $J = \Delta p$ to catches, kicks and landings; explain safety devices via the force–time argument.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Momentum is a vector — always assign signs/directions before computing $\Delta p$.</td></tr><tr><td>Impulse and momentum share units (N·s = kg·m/s); impulse is the area under the force–time graph.</td></tr><tr><td>For a FIXED momentum change, force and time trade off: $F_{avg} = \Delta p/\Delta t$.</td></tr><tr><td>$F = dp/dt$ is the general second law — essential for variable-mass (rocket) problems.</td></tr><tr><td>Momentum conservation holds in all collisions; kinetic energy only in elastic ones.</td></tr><tr><td>Recoil problems are momentum bookkeeping: $M V + m v = 0$ from rest.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Momentum and kinetic energy are interchangeable measures of motion.' ✅ Momentum $mv$ is a vector conserved in ALL collisions; kinetic energy $\frac{1}{2}mv^2$ is a scalar conserved only in elastic ones. Both grow with speed but differently.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Impulse equals force.' ✅ Impulse = force × time (or the area under $F$–$t$). A small force acting long can deliver a huge impulse.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The ball exerts no force on the bat while being hit because the bat is harder.' ✅ Newton's third law: contact forces are equal and opposite during the collision — the ball's velocity change comes from the contact force acting on IT.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'A heavy object always has more momentum.' ✅ Momentum = mass × VELOCITY — a light fast bullet can out-momentum a heavy slow trolley; direction matters too.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Using $F = ma$ for a rocket ejecting fuel. ✅ Mass changes with time, so $F = ma$ fails; the momentum form $F = dp/dt$ handles it and yields the rocket equation.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Impulse has units of newtons. ✅ N·s (= kg·m/s) — momentum units. Dimensionally $[MLT^{-1}]$.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Linear momentum $\vec{p} = m\vec{v}$ measures 'quantity of motion'. Newton's second law in its general form is $\vec{F} = d\vec{p}/dt$, and integrating it over a collision interval gives the impulse–momentum theorem $\vec{J} = \int F\,dt = \Delta\vec{p}$ — impulse is the area under the force–time graph. Because a given momentum change can come from a big force briefly or a small force slowly, safety design (airbags, crumple zones, bent knees) is the art of stretching $\Delta t$. Recoil, ball games and rocket thrust are all direct applications.</p><hr><h2>Conservation of Linear Momentum</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Law of Conservation of Linear Momentum:** In an isolated system (no external net force), the total linear momentum remains constant: $\sum \vec{p}_i = \sum \vec{p}_f$. This is a fundamental law derived from the homogeneity of space (Noether's theorem). It is a direct consequence of the fact that the laws of physics are the same at all points in space.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Isolated System:** A system where the net external force is zero. Internal forces (like collision forces) do not change the total momentum because they act in equal and opposite pairs (Newton's 3rd law). The system can be as small as two colliding particles or as large as the entire universe.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Coefficient of Restitution (e):** A measure of elasticity in collisions: $e = (v_2 - v_1) / (u_1 - u_2)$. $e=1$ (perfectly elastic), $0 < e < 1$ (inelastic), $e=0$ (perfectly inelastic). This coefficient depends on the materials of the colliding objects and their surface properties.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Collisions:** Events where momentum is conserved. Elastic collisions also conserve kinetic energy; inelastic collisions do not (energy is lost to heat, sound, deformation).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Explosions:** A reverse collision where an object breaks apart. Momentum is conserved, but kinetic energy increases (internal energy is converted to motion).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Recoil:** A classic application. A gun firing a bullet: $M_g V_g + m_b v_b = 0$ (initially at rest).</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** State the law; apply it to 1D and 2D collisions; distinguish between elastic and inelastic collisions; solve recoil and explosion problems.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Type 2 — alternate treatment:** this version derives the conservation law from Newton's third law and the impulse picture, then applies it to recoil, explosions and two-body systems — the application chain NEB numericals follow.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**The law:** if the net EXTERNAL force on a system is zero, its total linear momentum stays constant: $m_1\vec{u}_1 + m_2\vec{u}_2 = m_1\vec{v}_1 + m_2\vec{v}_2$. Internal forces (the pair between the two bodies) can change individual momenta but never the total.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Why it holds (third-law derivation):** during contact, body 1 pushes body 2 with $\vec{F}_{12}$ and body 2 pushes back $\vec{F}_{21} = -\vec{F}_{12}$ for the SAME time $\Delta t$. Impulses: $\Delta\vec{p}_1 = \vec{F}_{21}\Delta t$ and $\Delta\vec{p}_2 = \vec{F}_{12}\Delta t$; adding gives $\Delta(\vec{p}_1 + \vec{p}_2) = (\vec{F}_{21} + \vec{F}_{12})\Delta t = 0$ — the total momentum cannot change.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**When it applies:** always for INTERNAL interactions; for the whole system even during explosions and collisions (internal forces are huge compared to external ones over the short interaction). Gravity/friction are external — momentum is conserved only along directions with zero net external force.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Recoil (from rest):** initial momentum zero → $M\vec{V} + m\vec{v} = 0$, so $\vec{V} = -\dfrac{m}{M}\vec{v}$. Cannon–ball, rifle–bullet, rocket–exhaust, boat–passenger: the lighter body carries proportionally more speed.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Explosions and separations:** a body of mass $(m_1 + m_2)$ at rest splits into pieces moving in opposite directions: $m_1 v_1 = m_2 v_2$. Kinetic energy is NOT conserved here (chemical energy converts to motion) — momentum alone is the reliable bookkeeper.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>**Worked example:** a 60 kg skater at rest throws a 3 kg ball at 8 m/s. Recoil: $V = \frac{3 \times 8}{60} = 0.4$ m/s backward. If thrown at 30° above horizontal, only the horizontal component ($8\cos30°$) enters the horizontal recoil balance.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>**Collision bookkeeping:** in ANY collision (elastic or not) total momentum is conserved: $m_1u_1 + m_2u_2 = m_1v_1 + m_2v_2$ (1-D). Two unknowns need a second equation — kinetic energy (elastic) or the coefficient of restitution $e = \frac{v_2 - v_1}{u_1 - u_2}$.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** state the law with its condition (no net external force); derive it from the third law; solve recoil and explosion numericals; explain rocket propulsion and the gun recoil by momentum conservation.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Isolated system = no external force.</td></tr><tr><td>Momentum is always conserved in collisions.</td></tr><tr><td>Kinetic energy is conserved ONLY in elastic collisions.</td></tr><tr><td>Recoil and explosions are momentum conservation problems.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Momentum is conserved only in elastic collisions.' ✅ Momentum is conserved in ALL collisions (elastic or inelastic) as long as the system is isolated.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Kinetic energy is always conserved in collisions.' ✅ Only in perfectly elastic collisions. In inelastic collisions, KE is lost.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Internal forces change the total momentum.' ✅ Internal forces (like collision forces) cancel out in pairs (Newton's 3rd law) and do not change the total momentum of the system.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Conservation of momentum applies to individual objects.' ✅ It applies to the SYSTEM of objects.</td></tr><tr><td>Carefully distinguish between similar-looking concepts in conservation of linear momentum.</td></tr><tr><td>Pay attention to the conditions under which formulas and theorems apply.</td></tr><tr><td>Practice with multiple examples to solidify your understanding.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> The law of conservation of linear momentum states that the total momentum of an isolated system remains constant. It is a powerful tool for solving collision, explosion, and recoil problems. While momentum is conserved in all collisions, kinetic energy is only conserved in perfectly elastic ones. Conservation of Linear Momentum is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</p><hr><h2>Conservation of Linear Momentum</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Type 2 — alternate treatment:** this version emphasises the momentum form of Newton's second law and the impulse–momentum theorem with force–time graphs — the framing NEB theory questions use.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Linear momentum:** $\vec{p} = m\vec{v}$ — mass in motion, SI unit kg·m/s. It is a VECTOR along the velocity. A 1 kg ball at 10 m/s and a 10 kg cart at 1 m/s carry equal momentum but very different kinetic energies ($\frac{1}{2}mv^2$: 50 J vs 5 J).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Newton's second law in momentum form:** $\vec{F} = \dfrac{d\vec{p}}{dt} = \dfrac{d(m\vec{v})}{dt}$. For constant mass this reduces to $\vec{F} = m\vec{a}$ — but the momentum form is the GENERAL law and is the version that stays valid for variable-mass systems (rockets).</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Impulse:** $\vec{J} = \vec{F}\,\Delta t$ for a constant force, and $\vec{J} = \int \vec{F}\,dt$ in general — the AREA under a force–time graph. Impulse has units N·s = kg·m/s, the same as momentum — by design.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Impulse–momentum theorem:** $\vec{J} = \Delta\vec{p} = m\vec{v}_f - m\vec{v}_i$. Any desired momentum change can be had with a LARGE force for a short time or a SMALL force for a long time — the product is what matters.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Why airbags/crumple zones work:** to stop a passenger, $\Delta p$ is fixed by the crash; extending the collision time $\Delta t$ cuts the average force $F_{avg} = \Delta p/\Delta t$. Bending your knees on landing, catching a cricket ball with draw-back hands, and jumping with flexed legs are all the same physics.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Worked example:** a 0.15 kg cricket ball at 30 m/s is stopped in 0.1 s. $\Delta p = 0.15 \times 30 = 4.5$ kg·m/s → $F_{avg} = 45$ N. If caught in 0.02 s: 225 N — five times the force from the same momentum change.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Variable force:** when $F(t)$ is a curve, impulse is the area under it; for $F = F_0 e^{-t/\tau}$-style impacts, $J = F_0\tau$. NEB usually gives triangular or rectangular force–time pulses — count the areas.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** define momentum and impulse with units; state and use $F = dp/dt$; compute impulse from force–time graphs; apply $J = \Delta p$ to catches, kicks and landings; explain safety devices via the force–time argument.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Momentum is a vector — always assign signs/directions before computing $\Delta p$.</td></tr><tr><td>Impulse and momentum share units (N·s = kg·m/s); impulse is the area under the force–time graph.</td></tr><tr><td>For a FIXED momentum change, force and time trade off: $F_{avg} = \Delta p/\Delta t$.</td></tr><tr><td>$F = dp/dt$ is the general second law — essential for variable-mass (rocket) problems.</td></tr><tr><td>Momentum conservation holds in all collisions; kinetic energy only in elastic ones.</td></tr><tr><td>Recoil problems are momentum bookkeeping: $M V + m v = 0$ from rest.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Momentum and kinetic energy are interchangeable measures of motion.' ✅ Momentum $mv$ is a vector conserved in ALL collisions; kinetic energy $\frac{1}{2}mv^2$ is a scalar conserved only in elastic ones. Both grow with speed but differently.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Impulse equals force.' ✅ Impulse = force × time (or the area under $F$–$t$). A small force acting long can deliver a huge impulse.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The ball exerts no force on the bat while being hit because the bat is harder.' ✅ Newton's third law: contact forces are equal and opposite during the collision — the ball's velocity change comes from the contact force acting on IT.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'A heavy object always has more momentum.' ✅ Momentum = mass × VELOCITY — a light fast bullet can out-momentum a heavy slow trolley; direction matters too.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Using $F = ma$ for a rocket ejecting fuel. ✅ Mass changes with time, so $F = ma$ fails; the momentum form $F = dp/dt$ handles it and yields the rocket equation.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Impulse has units of newtons. ✅ N·s (= kg·m/s) — momentum units. Dimensionally $[MLT^{-1}]$.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Linear momentum $\vec{p} = m\vec{v}$ measures 'quantity of motion'. Newton's second law in its general form is $\vec{F} = d\vec{p}/dt$, and integrating it over a collision interval gives the impulse–momentum theorem $\vec{J} = \int F\,dt = \Delta\vec{p}$ — impulse is the area under the force–time graph. Because a given momentum change can come from a big force briefly or a small force slowly, safety design (airbags, crumple zones, bent knees) is the art of stretching $\Delta t$. Recoil, ball games and rocket thrust are all direct applications.</p><hr><h2>Application of Newton's Laws</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**The problem-solving framework (the 'FBD method'):** (1) isolate the body, (2) draw ALL forces acting ON it (weight, normal, friction, tension, applied), (3) choose axes (one along acceleration), (4) write $ΣF = ma$ for each axis, (5) solve. Every single NEB numerical follows this pattern.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Elevator weight paradox:** in an accelerating upward elevator, the normal force $N = m(g + a)$ — you feel heavier. Accelerating downward: $N = m(g - a)$ — you feel lighter. Free fall ($a = g$): $N = 0$ (weightlessness). The weight $mg$ never changes; what changes is the apparent weight (the normal force).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Atwood's machine (two masses on a pulley):** acceleration $a = \frac{(m_1 - m_2)g}{m_1 + m_2}$; tension $T = \frac{2m_1 m_2 g}{m_1 + m_2}$. The string is massless and inextensible; the pulley is frictionless — if not, extra terms appear.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Connected bodies (push and pull):** a single force F applied to a system of masses (m₁, m₂, m₃) gives $a = \frac{F}{m_1 + m_2 + m_3}$. The contact force between $m_1$ and $m_2$ is the force needed to accelerate the trailing masses: $N = (m_2 + m_3) \times a$.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Inclined plane:** weight components: mg sin θ down the slope, mg cos θ into the plane. Normal N = mg cos θ. With friction: net force down slope = $mg \sin\theta - \mu mg \cos\theta$. The body slides when $\sin\theta > \mu \cos\theta$, i.e., $\tan\theta > \mu$ — the critical angle depends only on $\mu$, NOT on mass.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Non-inertial frames and pseudo-forces:** When analyzing motion from an accelerating frame (e.g., inside an accelerating car), Newton's laws require the addition of a 'pseudo-force' $F_p = -ma_{frame}$ to account for the frame's acceleration. This force is not real (no agent), but it makes the math work.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Static vs. Kinetic Friction:** Static friction $f_s \le \mu_s N$ acts when there is no relative motion; kinetic friction $f_k = \mu_k N$ acts when sliding occurs. Always \mu_s > \mu_k.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Worked example:** a 5 kg block on a 30° rough slope (μ = 0.3) — does it slide? tan 30° = 0.577 > 0.3 → yes. Acceleration: a = g(sin 30° − 0.3 cos 30°) = 9.8(0.5 − 0.26) = 2.35 m/s² down the slope.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Apparent weight in fluids:** an object submerged in a liquid appears lighter by the buoyant force (Archimedes): apparent weight = mg − ρVg. This is still a Newton's second law application: ΣF = mg − ρVg = ma.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** elevator problems, Atwood's machine, connected bodies on horizontal surfaces, inclined plane with/without friction, apparent weight in accelerating systems — all use the same FBD → ΣF = ma framework.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**Newton's First Law (Inertia):** a body remains at rest or in uniform motion unless acted on by an external force. Inertia is the resistance to change in motion, quantified by mass. Mass is the measure of inertia — more massive objects are harder to accelerate.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Newton's Second Law:** $\vec{F}_{net} = m\vec{a} = \frac{d\vec{p}}{dt}$. The acceleration of a body is directly proportional to the net force and inversely proportional to its mass. In the form $\vec{F} = \frac{d\vec{p}}{dt}$, it is valid even when mass changes (e.g., rockets).</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Newton's Third Law:** for every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction. $\vec{F}_{AB} = -\vec{F}_{BA}$. Action and reaction forces act on DIFFERENT bodies, so they never cancel each other in a free-body diagram of a single body.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>**Free-body diagrams:** the key tool for applying Newton's laws. Draw the body of interest and show ALL forces acting ON it (not forces it exerts on others). Common forces: weight $mg$, normal reaction $N$, tension $T$, friction $f$.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>**Tension in a string:** for a massless, inextensible string, tension is the same throughout. For a massive string, tension varies along its length. At a pulley, the tension is the same on both sides if the pulley is massless and frictionless.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>**Normal reaction:** the force perpendicular to a surface. On an inclined plane at angle $\theta$, $N = mg\cos\theta$ (if no other vertical forces act). The normal force adjusts to prevent penetration of the surface.</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>**Pseudo force:** in a non-inertial frame accelerating with $\vec{a}$, a fictitious force $-m\vec{a}$ appears on every mass. For example, in an accelerating car, passengers feel pushed backward — this is the pseudo force. It has no reaction pair.</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>**Connected masses:** when two bodies are connected by a string over a pulley (Atwood machine), they share the same acceleration magnitude. Write $F = ma$ for each body separately and solve the system of equations.</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>**Friction in Newton's laws:** static friction $f_s \leq \mu_s N$ adjusts to prevent slipping; kinetic friction $f_k = \mu_k N$ opposes motion. Always include friction as a force in the free-body diagram when surfaces are rough.</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>**Apparent weight:** the normal force experienced by a person in an accelerating elevator. When accelerating upward, apparent weight $= m(g + a) > mg$. When accelerating downward, apparent weight $= m(g - a) < mg$. In free fall ($a = g$), apparent weight is zero — weightlessness.</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>**Atwood machine:** two masses $m_1$ and $m_2$ ($m_2 > m_1$) connected by a string over a frictionless pulley. Acceleration: $a = \frac{(m_2 - m_1)g}{m_1 + m_2}$. Tension: $T = \frac{2m_1 m_2 g}{m_1 + m_2}$. This is a standard problem type in NEB exams.</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>**Inclined plane:** a block of mass $m$ on a plane inclined at angle $\theta$. Resolve weight into components: $mg\sin\theta$ down the plane and $mg\cos\theta$ perpendicular to the plane. Normal reaction $N = mg\cos\theta$.</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>**System approach:** for connected bodies, you can sometimes treat the system as a whole. The net external force on the system equals total mass times acceleration: $F_{net, ext} = M_{total} \cdot a$. Internal forces (tension between connected bodies) cancel.</td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td>**Limiting friction:** the maximum static friction just before motion begins: $f_{s,max} = \mu_s N$. If the applied force exceeds this, the object starts moving and kinetic friction takes over: $f_k = \mu_k N < f_{s,max}$.</td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td>**Friction on an incline:** a block on an inclined plane begins to slide when $mg\sin\theta > \mu_s mg\cos\theta$, i.e., when $\tan\theta > \mu_s$. The critical angle is $\theta_c = \tan^{-1}\mu_s$, called the angle of repose.</td></tr><tr><td>26</td><td>**Pulley systems:** in a simple pulley, the tension is the same on both sides. In a block-and-tackle system with $n$ supporting strands, the mechanical advantage is $n$ — the force needed is $\frac{1}{n}$ of the weight (neglecting friction and pulley mass).</td></tr><tr><td>27</td><td>**Contact force between blocks:** when two blocks are pushed together on a frictionless surface, the contact force depends on how the external force is applied. If force $F$ pushes block $m_1$ which contacts $m_2$, the contact force on $m_2$ is $F \cdot \frac{m_2}{m_1 + m_2}$.</td></tr><tr><td>28</td><td>**Newton's laws in everyday life:** walking (friction pushes you forward), swimming (water pushes you forward as you push water backward), driving (friction between tyres and road provides forward force), jumping (ground pushes you up). All illustrate Newton's third law.</td></tr><tr><td>29</td><td>**Weightlessness in orbit:** astronauts in orbit are not weightless because gravity is absent — they are in continuous free fall around Earth. The normal force from the spacecraft floor is zero, giving the sensation of weightlessness.</td></tr><tr><td>30</td><td>**Non-inertial frames:** in an accelerating frame, Newton's second law must be modified: $\vec{F}_{net} + \vec{F}_{pseudo} = m\vec{a}_{rel}$. The pseudo force is $-m\vec{a}_{frame}$, where $\vec{a}_{frame}$ is the acceleration of the reference frame.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>The FBD method is universal: isolate → draw forces → choose axes → ΣF = ma → solve.</td></tr><tr><td>The normal force adapts to the situation — it is NOT always mg.</td></tr><tr><td>Tension in an accelerating string differs from the weight of the attached mass.</td></tr><tr><td>Action-reaction pairs act on different bodies — never cancel in a single FBD.</td></tr><tr><td>On a frictionless incline, all bodies slide at the same rate (independent of mass).</td></tr><tr><td>Pseudo-forces are necessary in non-inertial frames.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The normal force is always mg.' ✅ N = mg only on a horizontal surface with no vertical acceleration. On a slope N = mg cos θ; in an accelerating elevator N = m(g ± a).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Friction always opposes motion.' ✅ Friction opposes RELATIVE motion (or its tendency). Static friction can act in the direction of motion — e.g., the static friction on your feet pushes you forward when walking.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The tension in a string equals the weight of the hanging mass.' ✅ Only if the system is in equilibrium. In an accelerating Atwood's machine, T ≠ mg — use ΣF = ma on each mass to find T.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Action and reaction forces cancel each other.' ✅ They act on DIFFERENT bodies, so they never cancel in a single FBD. Include only forces acting ON the body you are analyzing.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Heavier bodies slide slower down a slope.' ✅ a = g(sin θ − μ cos θ) has no mass term — all bodies slide at the same rate (if μ is the same), just like free fall.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'A body at rest has no forces acting on it.' ✅ Static equilibrium means ΣF = 0, not zero forces — weight and normal (and possibly friction and tension) all act, they just balance.</td></tr><tr><td>Action-reaction pairs NEVER act on the same body — they act on two different bodies. A common mistake is to say "the book pushes down on the table and the table pushes up on the book, so they cancel" — these forces act on different bodies and never cancel for a single body's analysis.</td></tr><tr><td>Newton's second law is $\vec{F}_{net} = m\vec{a}$, not $\vec{F} = m\vec{a}$ for any single force. You must sum ALL forces acting on the body before setting them equal to $m\vec{a}$.</td></tr><tr><td>The normal reaction is NOT always equal to $mg$. On an inclined plane, $N = mg\cos\theta$. If an additional vertical force acts, $N$ changes. Always determine $N$ from the specific situation.</td></tr><tr><td>Tension is NOT a force that "saves" energy — it does no work on a system of connected masses because the displacements at both ends are equal (for an inextensible string), so the net work by tension is zero.</td></tr><tr><td>In an accelerating reference frame, pseudo forces are NOT real forces — they have no reaction pair and do not arise from any physical interaction. They are introduced only to make Newton's second law work in non-inertial frames.</td></tr><tr><td>A body moving at constant velocity has zero net force on it, but it can still have forces acting on it. The forces simply balance. A car moving at constant speed on a level road has engine force balancing friction and air resistance.</td></tr><tr><td>The tension in a string is NOT always equal to the weight of a hanging mass. If the mass is accelerating, $T = m(g \pm a)$. Only when the mass is at rest or moving at constant velocity does $T = mg$.</td></tr><tr><td>When two bodies of different masses are dropped from the same height, they fall with the same acceleration $g$ (neglecting air resistance). The gravitational force is larger on the heavier body, but its greater inertia exactly compensates.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Newton's second law (ΣF = ma) applied through free-body diagrams solves every mechanics problem: elevators (apparent weight), Atwood's machine, connected bodies, inclined planes (smooth and rough), and objects in fluids. The key is correct isolation of the body and accurate force identification. Pseudo-forces allow analysis in non-inertial frames.</p><hr><h2>Application of Newton's Laws</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**The problem-solving framework (the 'FBD method'):** (1) isolate the body, (2) draw ALL forces acting ON it (weight, normal, friction, tension, applied), (3) choose axes (one along acceleration), (4) write ΣF = ma for each axis, (5) solve. Every single NEB numerical follows this pattern.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Elevator weight paradox:** in an accelerating upward elevator, the normal force N = m(g + a) — you feel heavier. Accelerating downward: N = m(g − a) — you feel lighter. Free fall (a = g): N = 0 (weightlessness). The weight mg never changes; what changes is the apparent weight (the normal force).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Atwood's machine (two masses on a pulley):** acceleration a = (m₁ − m₂)g/(m₁ + m₂); tension T = 2m₁m₂g/(m₁ + m₂). The string is massless and inextensible; the pulley is frictionless — if not, extra terms appear.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Connected bodies (push and pull):** a single force F applied to a system of masses (m₁, m₂, m₃) gives a = F/(m₁ + m₂ + m₃). The contact force between m₁ and m₂ is the force needed to accelerate the trailing masses: N = (m₂ + m₃) × a.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Inclined plane:** weight components: mg sin θ down the slope, mg cos θ into the plane. Normal N = mg cos θ. With friction: net force down slope = mg sin θ − μmg cos θ. The body slides when sin θ > μ cos θ, i.e., tan θ > μ — the critical angle depends only on μ, NOT on mass.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Worked example:** a 5 kg block on a 30° rough slope (μ = 0.3) — does it slide? tan 30° = 0.577 > 0.3 → yes. Acceleration: a = g(sin 30° − 0.3 cos 30°) = 9.8(0.5 − 0.26) = 2.35 m/s² down the slope.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Apparent weight in fluids:** an object submerged in a liquid appears lighter by the buoyant force (Archimedes): apparent weight = mg − ρVg. This is still a Newton's second law application: ΣF = mg − ρVg = ma.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** elevator problems, Atwood's machine, connected bodies on horizontal surfaces, inclined plane with/without friction, apparent weight in accelerating systems — all use the same FBD → ΣF = ma framework.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>The FBD method is universal: isolate → draw forces → choose axes → ΣF = ma → solve.</td></tr><tr><td>The normal force adapts to the situation — it is NOT always mg.</td></tr><tr><td>Tension in an accelerating string differs from the weight of the attached mass.</td></tr><tr><td>Action-reaction pairs act on different bodies — never cancel in a single FBD.</td></tr><tr><td>On a frictionless incline, all bodies slide at the same rate (independent of mass).</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The normal force is always mg.' ✅ N = mg only on a horizontal surface with no vertical acceleration. On a slope N = mg cos θ; in an accelerating elevator N = m(g ± a).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Friction always opposes motion.' ✅ Friction opposes RELATIVE motion (or its tendency). Static friction can act in the direction of motion — e.g., the static friction on your feet pushes you forward when walking.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The tension in a string equals the weight of the hanging mass.' ✅ Only if the system is in equilibrium. In an accelerating Atwood's machine, T ≠ mg — use ΣF = ma on each mass to find T.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Action and reaction forces cancel each other.' ✅ They act on DIFFERENT bodies, so they never cancel in a single FBD. Include only forces acting ON the body you are analyzing.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Heavier bodies slide slower down a slope.' ✅ a = g(sin θ − μ cos θ) has no mass term — all bodies slide at the same rate (if μ is the same), just like free fall.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'A body at rest has no forces acting on it.' ✅ Static equilibrium means ΣF = 0, not zero forces — weight and normal (and possibly friction and tension) all act, they just balance.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Newton's second law (ΣF = ma) applied through free-body diagrams solves every mechanics problem: elevators (apparent weight), Atwood's machine, connected bodies, inclined planes (smooth and rough), and objects in fluids. The key is correct isolation of the body and accurate force identification.</p><hr><h2>Moment, Torque and Equilibrium</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Torque (moment of a force):** $\tau = rF \sin \theta$, where r is the distance from the pivot to the point of application, F is the force, and $\theta$ is the angle between r and F. The SI unit is Nm. Torque is positive for anticlockwise rotation, negative for clockwise.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Equilibrium of a rigid body:** two conditions must BOTH hold: (1) translational equilibrium: $\sum F = 0$ (no net force → no linear acceleration); (2) rotational equilibrium: $\sum \tau = 0$ (no net torque → no angular acceleration). A body at rest or rotating at constant angular velocity satisfies both.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Couple:** two equal and opposite forces separated by a distance d produce a pure torque (independent of pivot): $\tau = F \times d$. The net force is zero, so there is no linear acceleration — only rotation. The turning effect is the same about ANY point.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Centre of gravity (CG):** the single point where the entire weight of a body may be considered to act. For regular uniform bodies, the CG is at the geometric centre. For irregular bodies, it can be found by balancing or suspension.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Worked example — seesaw:** a 40 kg child sits 2 m from the fulcrum. Where must a 50 kg child sit to balance? Torque balance: $40 \times 9.8 \times 2 = 50 \times 9.8 \times x ightarrow x = 1.6 \text{ m}$. The heavier child sits closer to the fulcrum.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Worked example — ladder:** a uniform ladder of length L leans against a smooth wall at angle $\theta$. The wall reaction is horizontal (smooth), the ground reaction has both normal and friction components. Torque about the base eliminates the unknown ground reaction: $N_{\text{wall}} \times L \sin \theta = mg \times (L/2) \cos \theta$.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Equilibrium in the NEB exam:** the standard problem is a beam/ladder/plank with multiple forces — apply $\sum F_x = 0$, $\sum F_y = 0$, and $\sum \tau = 0$ (choosing the pivot wisely to eliminate unknowns). Three equations for up to three unknowns.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Practical examples:** opening a door (push at the handle, not near the hinge — larger r gives more $\tau$), using a spanner (longer handle = more torque for the same force), a wheelbarrow (load CG near the wheel reduces the lifting torque).</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Moment of a force (torque):** $\tau = rF\sin\theta$, where $r$ is the distance from the pivot to the point of application and $\theta$ is the angle between $\vec{r}$ and $\vec{F}$. The perpendicular distance $d = r\sin\theta$ is called the moment arm or lever arm.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Vector form:** $\vec{\tau} = \vec{r} \times \vec{F}$. Torque is a vector perpendicular to the plane containing $\vec{r}$ and $\vec{F}$, following the right-hand rule. Its magnitude is $\tau = rF\sin\theta$.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**Units:** torque has units of N·m. Note: while dimensionally equivalent to joules, torque is NOT energy and is never expressed in joules. Torque is a vector; energy is a scalar.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Couple:** two equal and opposite forces whose lines of action do not coincide. A couple produces pure rotation with zero net force, so it causes angular acceleration without translation.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Moment of a couple:** $\tau = F \cdot d$, where $d$ is the perpendicular distance between the lines of action. The moment of a couple is the same about ANY point — it is a free vector, independent of the reference point.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>**Equilibrium conditions:** for a rigid body to be in static equilibrium: (1) $\sum\vec{F} = 0$ (translational equilibrium) and (2) $\sum\vec{\tau} = 0$ about any point (rotational equilibrium). Both conditions must be satisfied simultaneously.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>**Types of equilibrium:** (1) Stable — body returns to equilibrium when displaced slightly (centre of gravity rises). (2) Unstable — body moves further from equilibrium when displaced (centre of gravity falls). (3) Neutral — body stays in new position (centre of gravity at same height).</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>**Center of gravity:** the point where the entire weight of the body may be considered to act. For a uniform body, it coincides with the geometrical center. For irregular bodies, it can be found experimentally by suspension.</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>**Principle of moments:** for a body in equilibrium, the sum of clockwise moments about any point equals the sum of anticlockwise moments about that point. This is a direct consequence of $\sum\vec{\tau} = 0$.</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>**Lever classes:** (1) Class 1: fulcrum between effort and load (seesaw). (2) Class 2: load between fulcrum and effort (wheelbarrow). (3) Class 3: effort between fulcrum and load (tweezers, human forearm). Mechanical advantage differs for each class.</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>**Calculating torque about a point:** choose a pivot point and calculate the torque of each force about that point. A force whose line of action passes through the pivot produces zero torque. Choose the pivot to eliminate unknown forces from the torque equation.</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>**Stability and center of gravity:** a body is stable when its center of gravity is low and its base of support is wide. Tipping occurs when the vertical line through the CG falls outside the base. This principle explains why racing cars have wide wheelbases and low centers of gravity.</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>**Torque and angular acceleration:** just as $F = ma$ governs translational motion, $\tau = I\alpha$ governs rotational motion, where $I$ is the moment of inertia and $\alpha$ is the angular acceleration.</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>**Static vs dynamic equilibrium:** static equilibrium means the body is at rest ($\vec{v} = 0$, $\vec{\omega} = 0$). Dynamic equilibrium means the body moves with constant velocity and constant angular velocity ($\vec{a} = 0$, $\vec{\alpha} = 0$). Both require $\sum\vec{F} = 0$ and $\sum\vec{\tau} = 0$.</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>**Finding the center of gravity experimentally:** suspend the body from two different points. The CG lies at the intersection of the vertical lines through each suspension point. This method works for any irregular shape.</td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td>**Torque balance in biological systems:** the human body uses lever systems extensively. The biceps muscle exerts a large force at a short distance from the elbow joint to lift a smaller weight at a longer distance — a class 3 lever with mechanical advantage less than 1.</td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td>**Torque and power:** rotational power is $P = \tau\omega$. For a given power, a larger torque produces slower rotation and vice versa. This is why bicycles use gear ratios — changing the effective torque at the wheel.</td></tr><tr><td>26</td><td>**Resultant of concurrent forces:** if multiple forces act on a body and their lines of action intersect at a point, you can find the resultant by vector addition. The body is in translational equilibrium if the resultant is zero.</td></tr><tr><td>27</td><td>**Non-concurrent forces:** when forces do not intersect at a single point, you must check both force equilibrium and torque equilibrium. A body can have zero net force but non-zero net torque (pure couple), causing rotation without translation.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Torque = $rF \sin \theta$ — it depends on force, distance, AND the angle between them.</td></tr><tr><td>Equilibrium requires BOTH $\sum F = 0$ and $\sum \tau = 0$ — one without the other is not enough.</td></tr><tr><td>A couple produces pure rotation (zero net force, non-zero torque) — the torque is the same about any point.</td></tr><tr><td>The centre of gravity simplifies a distributed weight to a single point force.</td></tr><tr><td>Choose the pivot at an unknown force's point of application to eliminate that force from the torque equation.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Torque and moment are different.' ✅ In physics problems, they are the same quantity: $\tau = rF \sin \theta$. Engineers sometimes distinguish 'moment' (bending) from 'torque' (twisting), but here they are interchangeable.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'A body with zero net force is in equilibrium.' ✅ It must ALSO have zero net torque. A couple (equal and opposite forces) produces zero net force but a non-zero torque — the body rotates.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The torque depends on the pivot chosen.' ✅ The torque of a single force depends on the pivot, but the NET torque in equilibrium is zero about ANY pivot. Choose the pivot to eliminate unknowns.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Heavier bodies always produce more torque.' ✅ Torque depends on BOTH force and distance. A small force far from the pivot can produce more torque than a large force close to it.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The centre of gravity is always inside the body.' ✅ For a ring, the CG is at the centre (in empty space). For an L-shaped body, it lies outside the material.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'A ladder leaning against a wall is safe if $\sum F = 0$.' ✅ You also need $\sum \tau = 0$ AND friction at the ground to be $\le \mu N$. The ladder slips when the required friction exceeds $\mu N_{\text{ground}}$.</td></tr><tr><td>Torque depends on the choice of pivot point, but the moment of a couple does NOT — it is the same about any point. This is because a couple produces pure rotation with no net force.</td></tr><tr><td>Zero net force does not imply zero net torque, and zero net torque does not imply zero net force. A body can be in rotational equilibrium but accelerating translationally, or vice versa.</td></tr><tr><td>The N·m unit for torque is NOT the same as the joule (energy), even though they are dimensionally equivalent. Torque is a vector (or pseudovector); energy is a scalar.</td></tr><tr><td>The point about which you calculate torque is arbitrary for equilibrium problems — if the body is in equilibrium, the net torque is zero about ANY point. Choose the point that minimizes the number of unknowns.</td></tr><tr><td>A body can have zero acceleration but non-zero torque if it is rotating with constant angular velocity — this is dynamic equilibrium, not static equilibrium.</td></tr><tr><td>In a class 3 lever (like a seesaw where the fulcrum is in the middle), the effort and load are on opposite sides. The mechanical advantage is less than 1, meaning you need more effort force than the load, but the load moves farther.</td></tr><tr><td>When calculating torque, only the component of force perpendicular to the position vector contributes. The parallel component ($F\cos\theta$) produces no torque about the pivot.</td></tr><tr><td>A body in stable equilibrium has its center of gravity at a minimum height. When displaced, the CG rises, and gravity provides a restoring torque that returns the body to equilibrium.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Torque ($\tau = rF \sin \theta$) is the rotational analogue of force. A rigid body is in equilibrium only when both $\sum F = 0$ (no linear acceleration) and $\sum \tau = 0$ (no angular acceleration). The principle of moments, couples, and centre of gravity are the key tools for solving beam, ladder, and structure problems. Moment, torque and equilibrium is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</p><hr><h2>Moment, Torque and Equilibrium</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Torque (moment of a force):** τ = rF sin θ, where r is the distance from the pivot to the point of application, F is the force, and θ is the angle between r and F. The SI unit is Nm. Torque is positive for anticlockwise rotation, negative for clockwise.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Equilibrium of a rigid body:** two conditions must BOTH hold: (1) translational equilibrium: ΣF = 0 (no net force → no linear acceleration); (2) rotational equilibrium: Στ = 0 (no net torque → no angular acceleration). A body at rest or rotating at constant angular velocity satisfies both.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Couple:** two equal and opposite forces separated by a distance d produce a pure torque (independent of pivot): τ = F × d. The net force is zero, so there is no linear acceleration — only rotation. The turning effect is the same about ANY point.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Centre of gravity (CG):** the single point where the entire weight of a body may be considered to act. For regular uniform bodies, the CG is at the geometric centre. For irregular bodies, it can be found by balancing or suspension.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Worked example — seesaw:** a 40 kg child sits 2 m from the fulcrum. Where must a 50 kg child sit to balance? Torque balance: 40×9.8×2 = 50×9.8×x → x = 1.6 m. The heavier child sits closer to the fulcrum.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Worked example — ladder:** a uniform ladder of length L leans against a smooth wall at angle θ. The wall reaction is horizontal (smooth), the ground reaction has both normal and friction components. Torque about the base eliminates the unknown ground reaction: N_wall × L sin θ = mg × (L/2) cos θ.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Equilibrium in the NEB exam:** the standard problem is a beam/ladder/plank with multiple forces — apply ΣF_x = 0, ΣF_y = 0, and Στ = 0 (choosing the pivot wisely to eliminate unknowns). Three equations for up to three unknowns.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Practical examples:** opening a door (push at the handle, not near the hinge — larger r gives more τ), using a spanner (longer handle = more torque for the same force), a wheelbarrow (load CG near the wheel reduces the lifting torque).</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Torque = rF sin θ — it depends on force, distance, AND the angle between them.</td></tr><tr><td>Equilibrium requires BOTH ΣF = 0 and Στ = 0 — one without the other is not enough.</td></tr><tr><td>A couple produces pure rotation (zero net force, non-zero torque) — the torque is the same about any point.</td></tr><tr><td>The centre of gravity simplifies a distributed weight to a single point force.</td></tr><tr><td>Choose the pivot at an unknown force's point of application to eliminate that force from the torque equation.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Torque and moment are different.' ✅ In physics problems, they are the same quantity: τ = rF sin θ. Engineers sometimes distinguish 'moment' (bending) from 'torque' (twisting), but here they are interchangeable.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'A body with zero net force is in equilibrium.' ✅ It must ALSO have zero net torque. A couple (equal and opposite forces) produces zero net force but a non-zero torque — the body rotates.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The torque depends on the pivot chosen.' ✅ The torque of a single force depends on the pivot, but the NET torque in equilibrium is zero about ANY pivot. Choose the pivot to eliminate unknowns.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Heavier bodies always produce more torque.' ✅ Torque depends on BOTH force and distance. A small force far from the pivot can produce more torque than a large force close to it.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The centre of gravity is always inside the body.' ✅ For a ring, the CG is at the centre (in empty space). For an L-shaped body, it lies outside the material.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'A ladder leaning against a wall is safe if ΣF = 0.' ✅ You also need Στ = 0 AND friction at the ground to be ≤ μN. The ladder slips when the required friction exceeds μN_ground.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Torque (τ = rF sin θ) is the rotational analogue of force. A rigid body is in equilibrium only when both ΣF = 0 (no linear acceleration) and Στ = 0 (no angular acceleration). The principle of moments, couples, and centre of gravity are the key tools for solving beam, ladder, and structure problems.</p><hr><h2>Solid Friction — Laws and Verification</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**What friction is:** a contact force that opposes relative motion (or its tendency) between two surfaces in contact. It acts parallel to the contact surface. Static friction (no sliding) and kinetic friction (sliding) are the two main types.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Laws of friction:** (1) Friction is independent of the apparent area of contact (to a good approximation). (2) Maximum static friction and kinetic friction are both proportional to the normal reaction: f_s(max) = μ_sN, f_k = μ_kN. (3) μ_k < μ_s — it is harder to start sliding than to keep sliding.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Static friction is a variable force:** it matches the applied force up to a maximum: f_s ≤ μ_sN. If you push with 30 N and the object doesn't move, f_s = 30 N (not μ_sN). Only at the point of slipping does f_s = μ_sN.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Angle of friction (λ):** the angle between the normal reaction and the resultant reaction (normal + friction) when slipping is about to occur. tan λ = μ_s. The resultant reaction makes angle λ with the normal.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Angle of repose (θ_r):** the steepest angle of a slope at which a body just remains at rest. At this angle, mg sin θ_r = μ_s mg cos θ_r → tan θ_r = μ_s. So θ_r = λ — the angle of repose equals the angle of friction.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Worked example — body on a slope:** a 10 kg block on a 25° slope (μ_s = 0.5). Does it slide? tan 25° = 0.466 < 0.5 → no, it stays. Static friction = mg sin 25° = 41.5 N (less than μ_sN = 0.5 × 92.4 = 46.2 N).</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Worked example — pulling a block:** a 5 kg block on a horizontal surface (μ_k = 0.3). Pulled by a 20 N force at 30° above horizontal. Normal N = mg − F sin 30° = 49 − 10 = 39 N. Friction = 0.3 × 39 = 11.7 N. Net force = 20 cos 30° − 11.7 = 5.6 N. a = 5.6/5 = 1.12 m/s².</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** distinguish static vs kinetic friction; solve slope problems (does it slide? find acceleration); angle of repose and its equality with angle of friction; pulling/pushing at an angle (changes the normal, hence friction).</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Static friction is VARIABLE: it matches the applied force up to μ_sN.</td></tr><tr><td>Kinetic friction is approximately CONSTANT: f_k = μ_kN during sliding.</td></tr><tr><td>μ_k < μ_s — harder to start sliding than to keep sliding.</td></tr><tr><td>Friction is (approximately) independent of the apparent area of contact.</td></tr><tr><td>Pulling at an angle reduces N and friction; pushing at an angle increases both.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Friction always opposes motion.' ✅ Friction opposes RELATIVE motion (or its tendency). Static friction can act IN the direction of motion — e.g., the static friction on your feet pushes you forward when walking.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Friction depends on the area of contact.' ✅ For a given normal force, friction is (approximately) independent of area. A wide tyre and a narrow tyre with the same load have the same maximum friction (in the simple model).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Static friction is always μ_sN.' ✅ Static friction is VARIABLE: it matches the applied force up to μ_sN. Only at the point of slipping does f_s = μ_sN.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'μ_k > μ_s because sliding is faster.' ✅ μ_k < μ_s — it is harder to start sliding than to keep sliding. This is why a heavy box jerks when you first push it.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The angle of repose depends on the mass of the body.' ✅ tan θ_r = μ_s — mass cancels out. All bodies with the same μ_s have the same angle of repose.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Pulling a block at an angle reduces friction because the force is smaller.' ✅ Pulling upward reduces the normal (N = mg − F sin θ), which reduces friction. Pushing downward increases the normal and friction.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Friction is a contact force opposing relative motion. Static friction (variable, up to μ_sN) acts before sliding; kinetic friction (constant, μ_kN) acts during sliding, with μ_k < μ_s. The angle of repose equals the angle of friction (tan⁻¹μ_s). Slope problems, pulling/pushing at angles, and the distinction between static and kinetic friction are the key NEB topics.</p><hr><h2>Solid Friction: Laws and Verification</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Definition of Solid Friction: Force opposing relative motion (or tendency of motion) between two solid surfaces in contact. Acts tangentially at the interface. Always opposes actual or impending motion.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Types of Solid Friction: (1) Static friction — acts when bodies are at rest relative to each other; self-adjusting up to a maximum value f_s(max) = μ_s N. (2) Kinetic (dynamic) friction — acts during sliding; constant magnitude f_k = μ_k N. (3) Rolling friction — much smaller than sliding friction.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Law 1 — Independence of Apparent Area: The force of friction is independent of the apparent area of contact, provided the normal reaction remains the same. A brick lying flat or on its edge experiences the same frictional force if weight is unchanged.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Law 2 — Proportionality to Normal Reaction: The limiting frictional force is directly proportional to the normal reaction between the surfaces: f_max = μN, where μ is the coefficient of friction. This defines μ as the ratio f_max/N.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Law 3 — Dependence on Nature of Surfaces: Friction depends on the materials and roughness of the contacting surfaces. Smooth glass-on-glass has low μ; rubber-on-concrete has high μ. Lubrication reduces μ dramatically.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Law 4 — Static vs Kinetic: Limiting static friction is greater than kinetic friction (μ_s > μ_k). Once motion begins, less force is needed to maintain it. This explains why starting a heavy object requires more effort than keeping it moving.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Law 5 — Direction of Friction: Friction always acts opposite to the direction of relative motion (kinetic) or impending motion (static). It is a passive force — it arises only in response to an applied force.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Verification by Horizontal Surface Method: Place a block on a horizontal table. Attach a spring balance horizontally. Pull gradually until the block just starts moving. Record the reading (= limiting friction f_max). Repeat with different weights on the block. Plot f_max vs N — straight line through origin confirms f ∝ N. Slope gives μ.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Verification by Inclined Plane Method: Place a block on an adjustable inclined plane. Gradually increase the angle θ until the block just begins to slide. At this critical angle (angle of repose), tan θ = μ. Measure θ for different surface combinations. Confirms μ depends on surface nature, not area or mass.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Coefficient of Friction Values: Steel on steel (dry): μ ≈ 0.6; wood on wood: μ ≈ 0.3–0.5; ice on ice: μ ≈ 0.03; rubber on dry concrete: μ ≈ 0.8; lubricated metal: μ ≈ 0.05.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Friction is independent of apparent contact area.</td></tr><tr><td>Limiting friction ∝ normal reaction (f_max = μN).</td></tr><tr><td>μ depends only on material pair and surface condition.</td></tr><tr><td>Static friction > kinetic friction (μ_s > μ_k).</td></tr><tr><td>Angle of repose θ satisfies tan θ = μ.</td></tr><tr><td>Friction always opposes relative or impending motion.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Thinking friction depends on contact area: It does NOT for dry friction — only normal reaction matters.</td></tr><tr><td>Confusing μ_s and μ_k: μ_s applies to stationary/impending motion; μ_k applies during sliding. μ_s ≥ μ_k always.</td></tr><tr><td>Believing friction always opposes motion: Static friction can act in the direction of motion (e.g., car tire pushing forward on road).</td></tr><tr><td>Assuming μ > 1 is impossible: μ can exceed 1 for very sticky surfaces (e.g., silicone rubber).</td></tr><tr><td>Forgetting friction is passive: It only exists in response to an applied force or tendency to move.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Solid friction opposes relative motion between surfaces in contact. The laws state that friction is independent of apparent area, proportional to normal reaction, and depends on surface nature. Static friction exceeds kinetic friction. Verified experimentally using inclined plane and horizontal surface methods.</p><hr><h2>Solid Friction</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**What friction is:** a contact force that opposes relative motion (or its tendency) between two surfaces in contact. It acts parallel to the contact surface. Static friction (no sliding) and kinetic friction (sliding) are the two main types.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Laws of friction:** (1) Friction is independent of the apparent area of contact (to a good approximation). (2) Maximum static friction and kinetic friction are both proportional to the normal reaction: f_s(max) = μ_sN, f_k = μ_kN. (3) μ_k < μ_s — it is harder to start sliding than to keep sliding.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Static friction is a variable force:** it matches the applied force up to a maximum: f_s ≤ μ_sN. If you push with 30 N and the object doesn't move, f_s = 30 N (not μ_sN). Only at the point of slipping does f_s = μ_sN.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Angle of friction (λ):** the angle between the normal reaction and the resultant reaction (normal + friction) when slipping is about to occur. tan λ = μ_s. The resultant reaction makes angle λ with the normal.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Angle of repose (θ_r):** the steepest angle of a slope at which a body just remains at rest. At this angle, mg sin θ_r = μ_s mg cos θ_r → tan θ_r = μ_s. So θ_r = λ — the angle of repose equals the angle of friction.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Worked example — body on a slope:** a 10 kg block on a 25° slope (μ_s = 0.5). Does it slide? tan 25° = 0.466 < 0.5 → no, it stays. Static friction = mg sin 25° = 41.5 N (less than μ_sN = 0.5 × 92.4 = 46.2 N).</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Worked example — pulling a block:** a 5 kg block on a horizontal surface (μ_k = 0.3). Pulled by a 20 N force at 30° above horizontal. Normal N = mg − F sin 30° = 49 − 10 = 39 N. Friction = 0.3 × 39 = 11.7 N. Net force = 20 cos 30° − 11.7 = 5.6 N. a = 5.6/5 = 1.12 m/s².</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** distinguish static vs kinetic friction; solve slope problems (does it slide? find acceleration); angle of repose and its equality with angle of friction; pulling/pushing at an angle (changes the normal, hence friction).</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Friction:** the force that opposes relative motion (or impending motion) between two surfaces in contact. It arises from microscopic irregularities and molecular adhesion between surfaces.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Coefficient of friction $\mu$:** $\mu = \frac{F}{R}$, where $F$ is the limiting frictional force and $R$ is the normal reaction. $\mu$ is dimensionless — it is a ratio of two forces.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**Laws of friction:** (1) Friction acts opposite to the direction of impending motion. (2) Limiting friction $F_s = \mu_s R$, proportional to the normal reaction. (3) Friction is independent of the apparent area of contact (for a given normal force). (4) Friction depends on the nature and condition of the surfaces in contact.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Static vs kinetic friction:** static friction adjusts up to a maximum $F_{s,max} = \mu_s R$; kinetic friction is approximately constant at $F_k = \mu_k R$, where typically $\mu_s > \mu_k$. This is why it is harder to start moving an object than to keep it moving.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Angle of friction $\lambda$:** the angle between the resultant contact force and the normal reaction when limiting friction is reached. $\tan\lambda = \mu_s$. When the incline angle equals the angle of friction, the body is on the point of sliding.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>**Angle of repose:** the maximum angle of an incline at which a body just rests on the plane without sliding. It equals the angle of friction: $\theta_{repose} = \lambda = \tan^{-1}\mu_s$.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>**Verification of area independence:** experiments show that for a given normal force, doubling the contact area halves the pressure but leaves the frictional force unchanged because the increased number of molecular interactions compensates.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>**Rolling friction:** much smaller than sliding friction because rolling deforms surfaces less and the contact time per point is shorter. Ball bearings use this principle to reduce friction.</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>**Units check:** $\mu = F/R$ — both numerator and denominator are forces, so $\mu$ is dimensionless. Friction force $F = \mu R$ has units of newtons.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Static friction is VARIABLE: it matches the applied force up to μ_sN.</td></tr><tr><td>Kinetic friction is approximately CONSTANT: f_k = μ_kN during sliding.</td></tr><tr><td>μ_k < μ_s — harder to start sliding than to keep sliding.</td></tr><tr><td>Friction is (approximately) independent of the apparent area of contact.</td></tr><tr><td>Pulling at an angle reduces N and friction; pushing at an angle increases both.</td></tr><tr><td>• Solid friction</td></tr><tr><td>• Laws of solid friction</td></tr><tr><td>• Their verifications</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Friction always opposes motion.' ✅ Friction opposes RELATIVE motion (or its tendency). Static friction can act IN the direction of motion — e.g., the static friction on your feet pushes you forward when walking.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Friction depends on the area of contact.' ✅ For a given normal force, friction is (approximately) independent of area. A wide tyre and a narrow tyre with the same load have the same maximum friction (in the simple model).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Static friction is always μ_sN.' ✅ Static friction is VARIABLE: it matches the applied force up to μ_sN. Only at the point of slipping does f_s = μ_sN.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'μ_k > μ_s because sliding is faster.' ✅ μ_k < μ_s — it is harder to start sliding than to keep sliding. This is why a heavy box jerks when you first push it.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The angle of repose depends on the mass of the body.' ✅ tan θ_r = μ_s — mass cancels out. All bodies with the same μ_s have the same angle of repose.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Pulling a block at an angle reduces friction because the force is smaller.' ✅ Pulling upward reduces the normal (N = mg − F sin θ), which reduces friction. Pushing downward increases the normal and friction.</td></tr><tr><td>Friction is NOT always opposite to the direction of motion — it is opposite to the direction of relative motion (or impending relative motion) between the two surfaces. For example, when you walk, friction on your foot points forward (in the direction of motion), preventing your foot from slipping backward.</td></tr><tr><td>The friction force is NOT always $\mu R$. This formula gives the LIMITING (maximum static) friction. For static friction, $f_s \leq \mu_s R$, and the actual value adjusts to match the applied force up to the maximum.</td></tr><tr><td>Friction being independent of area is an approximation that holds for rigid bodies with moderate contact pressures. For very soft materials (like rubber tyres) or very high pressures, the relationship becomes more complex.</td></tr><tr><td>$\mu_s > \mu_k$ is generally true but not universal. Some materials (like Teflon on Teflon) have nearly equal coefficients, and certain combinations can even have $\mu_k > \mu_s$ under specific conditions.</td></tr><tr><td>The normal reaction $R$ is NOT always equal to $mg$. On an inclined plane, $R = mg\cos\theta$. If an additional vertical force is applied, $R$ changes accordingly. Always determine $R$ from force equilibrium in the direction perpendicular to the surface.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Friction is a contact force opposing relative motion. Static friction (variable, up to μ_sN) acts before sliding; kinetic friction (constant, μ_kN) acts during sliding, with μ_k < μ_s. The angle of repose equals the angle of friction (tan⁻¹μ_s). Slope problems, pulling/pushing at angles, and the distinction between static and kinetic friction are the key NEB topics. Solid Friction: Laws of Friction & Their Verification covers essential principles and applications. Friction is a contact force opposing relative motion. Static friction (variable, up to μ_sN) acts before sliding; kinetic friction (constant, μ_kN) acts during sliding, with μ_k < μ_s. The angle of repose equals the angle of friction (tan⁻¹μ_s). Slope problems, pulling/pushing at angles, and the distinction between static and kinetic friction are the key NEB topics. Solid friction: laws of solid friction and their verifications is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</p><hr><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: Linear Momentum and Impulse</td><td>Linear momentum $\vec{p} = m\vec{v}$ measures 'quantity of motion'. Newton's second law in its general form is $\vec{F} = d\vec{p}/dt$, and integrating it over a collision interval gives the impulse–momentum theorem $\vec{J} = \int F\,dt = \Delta\vec{p}$ — impulse is the area under the force–time graph. Because a given momentum change can come from a big force briefly or a small force slowly, safety design (airbags, crumple zones, bent knees) is the art of stretching $\Delta t$. Recoil, ball games and rocket thrust are all direct applications.</td></tr><tr><td>2: Conservation of Linear Momentum</td><td>The law of conservation of linear momentum states that the total momentum of an isolated system remains constant. It is a powerful tool for solving collision, explosion, and recoil problems. While momentum is conserved in all collisions, kinetic energy is only conserved in perfectly elastic ones. Conservation of Linear Momentum is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</td></tr><tr><td>3: Conservation of Linear Momentum</td><td>Linear momentum $\vec{p} = m\vec{v}$ measures 'quantity of motion'. Newton's second law in its general form is $\vec{F} = d\vec{p}/dt$, and integrating it over a collision interval gives the impulse–momentum theorem $\vec{J} = \int F\,dt = \Delta\vec{p}$ — impulse is the area under the force–time graph. Because a given momentum change can come from a big force briefly or a small force slowly, safety design (airbags, crumple zones, bent knees) is the art of stretching $\Delta t$. Recoil, ball games and rocket thrust are all direct applications.</td></tr><tr><td>4: Application of Newton's Laws</td><td>Newton's second law (ΣF = ma) applied through free-body diagrams solves every mechanics problem: elevators (apparent weight), Atwood's machine, connected bodies, inclined planes (smooth and rough), and objects in fluids. The key is correct isolation of the body and accurate force identification. Pseudo-forces allow analysis in non-inertial frames.</td></tr><tr><td>5: Application of Newton's Laws</td><td>Newton's second law (ΣF = ma) applied through free-body diagrams solves every mechanics problem: elevators (apparent weight), Atwood's machine, connected bodies, inclined planes (smooth and rough), and objects in fluids. The key is correct isolation of the body and accurate force identification.</td></tr><tr><td>6: Moment, Torque and Equilibrium</td><td>Torque ($\tau = rF \sin \theta$) is the rotational analogue of force. A rigid body is in equilibrium only when both $\sum F = 0$ (no linear acceleration) and $\sum \tau = 0$ (no angular acceleration). The principle of moments, couples, and centre of gravity are the key tools for solving beam, ladder, and structure problems. Moment, torque and equilibrium is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</td></tr><tr><td>7: Moment, Torque and Equilibrium</td><td>Torque (τ = rF sin θ) is the rotational analogue of force. A rigid body is in equilibrium only when both ΣF = 0 (no linear acceleration) and Στ = 0 (no angular acceleration). The principle of moments, couples, and centre of gravity are the key tools for solving beam, ladder, and structure problems.</td></tr><tr><td>8: Solid Friction — Laws and Verification</td><td>Friction is a contact force opposing relative motion. Static friction (variable, up to μ_sN) acts before sliding; kinetic friction (constant, μ_kN) acts during sliding, with μ_k < μ_s. The angle of repose equals the angle of friction (tan⁻¹μ_s). Slope problems, pulling/pushing at angles, and the distinction between static and kinetic friction are the key NEB topics.</td></tr><tr><td>9: Solid Friction: Laws and Verification</td><td>Solid friction opposes relative motion between surfaces in contact. The laws state that friction is independent of apparent area, proportional to normal reaction, and depends on surface nature. Static friction exceeds kinetic friction. Verified experimentally using inclined plane and horizontal surface methods.</td></tr><tr><td>10: Solid Friction</td><td>Friction is a contact force opposing relative motion. Static friction (variable, up to μ_sN) acts before sliding; kinetic friction (constant, μ_kN) acts during sliding, with μ_k < μ_s. The angle of repose equals the angle of friction (tan⁻¹μ_s). Slope problems, pulling/pushing at angles, and the distinction between static and kinetic friction are the key NEB topics. Solid Friction: Laws of Friction & Their Verification covers essential principles and applications. Friction is a contact force opposing relative motion. Static friction (variable, up to μ_sN) acts before sliding; kinetic friction (constant, μ_kN) acts during sliding, with μ_k < μ_s. The angle of repose equals the angle of friction (tan⁻¹μ_s). Slope problems, pulling/pushing at angles, and the distinction between static and kinetic friction are the key NEB topics. Solid friction: laws of solid friction and their verifications is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</td></tr></table></body></html>